<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Suspected endometriosis in a 30-year-old actor with severe painful periods since her teens, pelvic pain between periods, deep pain with intercourse, and painful bowel movements during periods, who wants children in a year or two. She started naproxen and a continuous combined pill on the day of this referral. Please confirm the diagnosis, advise on hormonal treatment and on whether and when surgery would help, and counsel her about fertility. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks, on the day of her transvaginal ultrasound. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does she have endometriosis, and is a transvaginal ultrasound enough to diagnose it, or is a laparoscopy needed?</item><item>Is the continuous combined pill the right first treatment, given that she wants a pregnancy in about a year, and what should follow if it fails?</item><item>Would surgery help her now, and how should the risks of ovarian surgery to her fertility be weighed?</item><item>How should her fertility be assessed and her pregnancy planned?</item><item>What non-drug treatments would help her pain, and do you agree with avoiding opioids and sedatives?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Painful periods since her teens, worse over the past three years; she misses two or three working days each cycle. Pain also between periods, deep pain with intercourse, and painful bowel movements during periods; no bleeding between periods or after intercourse. No previous pregnancies; condoms until now; not wanting a pregnancy for at least a year. Examination today: tender, slightly fixed retroverted uterus with left adnexal fullness and tenderness along the left uterosacral ligament. Urine pregnancy test negative; gonorrhea and chlamydia tests and urinalysis sent. Hemoglobin 12.2 g/dL, white cells 6.4 and platelets 262 x10^3/uL. Pain 8 of 10 at worst. She has used ibuprofen without relief, six hydrocodone tablets from urgent care two months ago, and diphenhydramine to sleep on bad nights; the prescription monitoring program shows only that opioid prescription. We have agreed on no opioid, benzodiazepine or sleep-medicine prescriptions. PHQ-9 12 (item 9 scored 0) and GAD-7 8: a moderate depressive episode, now starting cognitive behavioral therapy, with a safety plan and no thoughts of suicide or self-harm. Blood pressure 112/70; non-smoker; no migraine with aura or clotting history; started ethinyl estradiol 0.02 mg with norethindrone acetate 1 mg daily without a break, and naproxen 500 mg twice daily with food on pain days. She decides who is told about her health [S]</paragraph><list><item>Her periods are so painful that she misses work, and she wants to know what is wrong. [S]</item><item>She wants children one day and is afraid that the cause of her pain, or an operation, will prevent it. [S]</item><item>She feels low and sleeps badly on painful nights, and does not want to depend on pills. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>She was born Norma Jeane Mortenson on June 1, 1926, at Los Angeles General Hospital. Her father&apos;s identity is unconfirmed; her mother was often confined to asylums, and she spent her childhood in orphanages and foster homes. [D]</item><item>She worked as a photographic model, signed with Twentieth Century-Fox in 1946 under the name Marilyn Monroe, starred in films of the 1950s including Gentlemen Prefer Blondes and The Seven Year Itch, and studied at the Actors Studio in the mid-1950s. [D]</item><item>Her mother, Gladys, was diagnosed with paranoid schizophrenia and was institutionalized, for the first time at the state hospital in Norwalk. [D]</item><item>She is described as one of the most famous people with endometriosis. Her biographer Anthony Summers wrote that the condition was so severe that it destroyed her marriages, her wish for children, her career and ultimately her life; a review of endometriosis for clinicians quotes this account, and museum and medical-history writers list endometriosis among her health problems. [D]</item><item>The same biographical account holds that, in the days before effective conservative surgery or medical therapy, her endometriosis led to progressively increasing use of strong analgesics, tranquilizers and hypnotics, and to drug dependency. [R]</item><item>The physician and historian Howard Markel writes that she had several mental health problems, including substance abuse, depression and, most likely, bipolar disorder, as well as endometriosis and gall bladder disease. [R]</item><item>Most of the drugs she misused had been prescribed to her by physicians; they included the barbiturate Nembutal and chloral hydrate. [D]</item><item>A retrospective psychological autopsy based on biographies, films and her writings proposes autism spectrum traits progressing to borderline personality disorder and bipolar disorder; the roster lists borderline personality disorder and depression. [R]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

